      <section class="panel view-panel" data-view="about">
        <h2>About</h2>
        <div class="about-body">
          <p>The <strong>Symmetric Cryptography Database</strong> is a structured, curated collection of symmetric-key designs, split into two tiers: <strong>primitives</strong> — fixed-length constructions with no direct standalone cryptographic use (permutations, block ciphers, tweakable block ciphers, compression/update functions) — and <strong>modes</strong> — variable-length constructions directly usable as a cryptographic algorithm (AEAD, hash, XOF, MAC, KDF, PBKDF, PRNG, key wrap, stream cipher). A mode is typically built on top of one or more primitives (e.g. AES-GCM wraps the AES block cipher in the GCM construction; Ascon-Hash wraps the Ascon-p permutation in a sponge). Each side carries its own design lineage, construction taxonomy, and participation in standardisation processes.</p>
          <p>The data is stored in a SQLite database built from hand-curated JSON source files. Families are linked by explicit influence edges (e.g. <em>same S-box</em>, <em>improvement of</em>, <em>variant of</em>) which make up the genealogy graph — primitive and mode families share one id space, so an edge can cross tiers (e.g. a mode influenced by the primitive it wraps). The <strong>Timelines</strong> tab places each family on a publication-year axis grouped by any chosen dimension; the <strong>Genealogy</strong> tab renders the influence graph as a layered DAG or as a radial tree. Both tabs share the same filtering, split into a Fixed-length primitives section and a Variable-length modes section — each with its own toggle and its own Types / Constructions / Target applications / Processes checklists, so a construction like "Duplex Mode" (mode) is never mixed into the same list as "SPN" (primitive).</p>
          <p>Families are annotated with:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Construction</strong> — for primitives: SPN, Feistel, ARX, Even-Mansour, etc.; for modes: sponge/duplex, Merkle-Damgard, GCM, HMAC, etc.</li>
            <li><strong>Type</strong> — for primitives: permutation, block cipher, tweakable block cipher, compression/update function; for modes: AEAD, hash, XOF, MAC, KDF, PBKDF, PRNG, key wrap, stream cipher.</li>
            <li><strong>Standardisation process</strong> — AES, SHA-3, CAESAR, NIST-LWC, …</li>
            <li><strong>Design components</strong> — S-boxes, permutation layers, MixColumns, key schedules.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="small-note">Data is updated manually. The site is generated from source by a Python build pipeline and contains no server-side logic.</p>
          <p class="small-note">For precise definitions of every tier, type, construction, component, target application, process, and relation used here, see the <strong>Glossary</strong> tab above (or its <a href="https://github.com/peacker/symmetric_cryptography_database/blob/main/GLOSSARY.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Markdown source</a> on GitHub).</p>
        </div>
